INT. CAROUSEL FACTORY, PAINTING LOFT - NIGHT

Wooden horses hang from the rafters on hooks, half-painted, mouths open. Brass poles in racks. Smell of varnish.

FOX, 30s, crew boss, kneels at a floor safe behind a stacked pile of tarps. MABEL OSEI, 50s, safecracker, presses a stethoscope to the dial. KWAME, 20s, wheelman, stands at the window watching the street. ISOLDE, 30s, the inside woman, in a cleaner's smock, holds a mop she does not use.

                    FOX
          Three minutes, people.

                    MABEL
          (muttering)
          Left. Right. Come on, baby.

                    KWAME
          Cop car. Slow roll.

                    ISOLDE
          They check this block every night.

                    FOX
          Kwame, lights.

He kills the lamp. Dark, except for a wedge of streetlight on the horses.

                    MABEL
          Two clicks.

                    KWAME
          They're stopping.

                    ISOLDE
          They're not stopping, they're

                    FOX
          Quiet!

                    MABEL
          Three clicks. I've got it. I

                    KWAME
          They stopped!

Everyone freezes. A flashlight beam crosses a painted horse, pauses on its glass eye, and moves on. Footsteps outside. A radio squawk. Then the car pulls away, tires hissing.

Fox exhales. Kwame laughs once, a cracked bark.

                    MABEL
          Safe's open.

                    ISOLDE
          (to herself)
          Thank God.

                    FOX
          (to all)
          Grab the bags, we go in ninety.
